# Security Rules

This platform handles national ID numbers, tax identifiers, and money.
Treat every rule below as a hard requirement, not a suggestion to weigh
against convenience.

## Authentication & authorization

- Auth.js v5 config lives in a single `auth.ts` exporting
  `handlers`/`auth`/`signIn`/`signOut`. Don't recreate the older
  `[...nextauth]` options-object pattern from v4 — they're structurally
  different and mixing patterns causes type errors that look unrelated to auth.
- `middleware.ts` is the authorization gate for route groups. A page
  component checking `session.user.role` and conditionally rendering is
  **not** sufficient access control on its own — the middleware check is
  what actually prevents access, the page-level check is just UX.
- Domain and API-layer code never trusts a role, userId, or permission
  flag passed from client input — always re-derive identity from the
  server-side session.

## Sensitive data

- CNIC and NTN are encrypted at rest (AES-256-GCM, via
  `packages/database/encryption.ts`) and **never** appear in plaintext in:
  logs, error messages, analytics events, or test fixtures committed to
  the repo. Use realistic but clearly-fake patterns in tests/seed data.
- When displaying a CNIC in the UI outside an explicitly-authorized admin
  view, mask it (`35202-XXXXX-X`).
- Key rotation strategy is documented in ADR-005 — if you're touching the
  encryption helper, check that ADR before changing the key-derivation approach.

## Input validation

- Every boundary — tRPC procedure, Server Action, webhook handler — has a
  Zod schema validating input before any business logic runs. Reject
  early with a clear error; don't validate halfway through a function body.
- Webhook handlers (escrow, verification callbacks) verify the request's
  signature/origin before trusting the payload — never process a webhook
  body as authoritative without that check.

## Secrets & environment variables

- All env vars are validated at boot via the Zod schema in
  `packages/config/env.ts`. If you add a new required env var, add it to
  that schema in the same change — don't let the app start successfully
  with a silently-undefined secret.
- Never commit a real credential, even temporarily "to test" — use the
  mock verification adapters for local development.

## Rate limiting

- Auth endpoints, booking-token payment endpoints, and any
  verification-triggering endpoint (NADRA/FBR calls) are rate-limited via
  `@upstash/ratelimit`. If you add a new endpoint in one of these
  categories, add the rate limit alongside it — don't ship it unprotected
  and plan to add limiting "later."

## Headers

- Security headers (CSP, `X-Frame-Options`, `Strict-Transport-Security`,
  `Referrer-Policy`) are set centrally in `next.config.ts`. Don't override
  or weaken them in an individual route for convenience — fix the root
  config if a legitimate need conflicts with a header.
